ain. The treatment is contraindicated in patients with conditions such as myasthenia gravis or amyotrophic lateral sclerosis due to the potential for exacerbating neuromuscular dysfunction. Its use requires trained clinicians and careful patient selection. [Data: Entities (214); Relationships (313, 235, 236, 294, +more)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"A-type botulinum toxin is a high-evidence, first-line preventive treatment for chronic migraine with specific administration protocols and notable safety considerations\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Topiramate (托吡酯), an antiepileptic drug, is a well-established preventive agent for chronic migraine, with a recommended daily dose range of 25 to 200 mg. Multiple controlled trials have confirmed its efficacy, with evidence suggesting it may be as effective as amitriptyline, another commonly used preventive medication. However, its use is limited by a range of adverse effects, including paresthesia (tingling sensations), cognitive impairment, weight loss, dizziness, and difficulty with coordination. These side effects can significantly impact patient adherence and quality of life, necessitating careful titration and monitoring. Despite these challenges, topiramate remains a key pharmacological option due to its proven effectiveness and availability. [Data: Entities (181); Relationships (236, 235, 321, 307, +more)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Topiramate is an effective but side-effect-prone preventive medication for chronic migraine, requiring careful clinical management\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Valproate (丙戊酸盐), another antiepileptic drug, is a well-documented preventive treatment for migraine, with a recommended daily dose of 500 to 1500 mg. It has been validated through multiple randomized, placebo-controlled trials, demonstrating its ability to reduce migraine frequency. However, its use is associated with several significant side effects, including weight ga