, and patient preferences. Preventive strategies often involve a stepwise approach, starting with first-line agents such as A-type botulinum toxin or topiramate, followed by alternatives like valproate or tizanidine if initial treatments fail or are poorly tolerated. The integration of non-pharmacological interventions—such as lifestyle modifications, cognitive behavioral therapy, and stress management—is also recommended to enhance outcomes. [Data: Entities (104, 214, 181, 180, 213); Relationships (321, 313, 236, 235, +more)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Chronic migraine management demands a personalized, multidimensional strategy that integrates pharmacological and non-pharmacological approaches\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"The evidence base for chronic migraine treatments varies significantly across medications. A-type botulinum toxin and topiramate are supported by high-level evidence (Grade A) and strong recommendations (Level I), making them first-line choices. Valproate also has strong evidence (Grade A), though its risk profile limits its use. Tizanidine, in contrast, is supported by lower-level evidence (Grade C), indicating less certainty in its effectiveness. This hierarchy influences clinical guidelines and treatment algorithms, with higher-evidence drugs typically prioritized. The presence of multiple treatment options with varying evidence levels underscores the need for ongoing research and individualized decision-making. [Data: Entities (214, 181, 180, 213); Relationships (313, 236, 235, 307, +more)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Treatment options for chronic migraine vary in evidence strength, influencing clinical prioritization and guideline recommendations\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Table 11 (表11) serves as a central reference document summarizing the key characteristics of preventive medications for chronic migraine. It systematically lists each drug’s recommended strength, evidence level, dosage range, major side effects, and contraindications. This structured format enables clinicians to compare treatment options efficiently and make informed decisions. The table includes data on a range of agents, including botulinum toxin, topiramate, valproate, tizanidine, and others such as atenolol, which is also referenced in the table. Its inclusion in the knowledge base highlights its importance as a clinical decision-support tool. [Data: Entities (215, 294); Relationships (294, 321, 313, 307, +more)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Table 11 is a critical reference tool that consolidates evidence-based recommendations for chronic migraine prevention\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"The relationship between antiepileptic drugs (AEDs) and migraine prevention is well-established, with both topiramate and valproate being members of this class. These drugs are used off-label for migraine prevention despite their primary indication for epilepsy. Their mechanism of action in migraine is thought to involve modulation of neuronal excitability and cortical spreading depression. The fact that two AEDs are first-line treatments for chronic migraine underscores the shared pathophysiological pathways between epilepsy and migraine. This connection supports the use of AEDs in migraine management and informs future research into neuroprotective and neuromodulatory therapies. [Data: Entities (179, 181, 180); Relationships (233, 235, 236, 321, +more)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Antiepileptic drugs are a cornerstone of chronic migraine prevention, reflecting shared neurobiological mechanisms between epilepsy and migraine\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Eptinezumab, a monoclonal antibody targeting calcitonin gene-related peptide (CGRP), is recommended for the prevention of chronic migraine. It is administered intravenously and has demonstrated significant efficacy in reducing migraine days in clinical trials. Its inclusion 